// `CallExternalNative`, SPLIT OUT BECAUSE THE HARNESS CANNOT DRIVE IT.
//
// The differential registers `host::tick` as a VERIFIED native, and a module
// declaring `use external host::tick` emits `CallExternalNative`. The virtual
// machine refuses that combination at load:
//
//   "native `host::tick` registered as verified but bytecode invokes it as
//    external"
//
// So a module using an external native cannot be EXECUTED by this harness at
// all, whatever the backend does with it. THAT IS A HARNESS FACT, NOT A BACKEND
// REFUSAL -- `CallExternalNative` lowers, and the opcode census reports it as
// lowered.
//
// IT WAS ALSO NOT THE REASON I EXPECTED. `opcode_witness.kel` was split to make
// its arithmetic executable, on the assumption that `FixedDiv`, `Len` and
// `IsStruct` were the only things holding it out of the comparison. They were
// not: this native held it out too, and the split surfaced that rather than
// fixing it. The assumption was the finding.
//
// Kept as its own file so the opcode retains a corpus witness while the
// programs that CAN execute are free to.
//
// UPDATE 2026-09-16: THE OBSTRUCTION WAS THE REGISTRATION, AND IT IS REMOVABLE.
// Everything above remains true OF THIS HARNESS -- the corpus differential still
// registers every native as verified, so this module still cannot execute there.
// What was wrong was the implied generality. `Vm::register_external_native`
// exists, and registering the native the way its declaration asks makes the
// module executable on both implementations. `driven_opcode_witnesses.rs` now
// drives `CallExternalNative` end to end against a non-identity stub, so the
// opcode has a DRIVEN witness and no longer rests on this citation alone.
//
// WITNESSES: CallExternalNative
//
// THE LINE ABOVE IS A MACHINE-CHECKED CLAIM, not a comment.
// `witness_integrity.rs` compiles this file and asserts every opcode named
// there is actually emitted. A witness that silently STOPS witnessing --
// because the reference compiler folded the construct away, or because a
// function moved to another file -- would otherwise drop a census figure
// with nothing going red.
//
// That is not hypothetical: the `Op::IsStruct` construct was repaired out of
// existence by the `v0.2.3` line one hour after this corpus adopted it.

use external host::tick

fn main(seed: Word) -> Word {
  host::tick(seed)
}
